## Objective

Produce one self-contained document that a person picking this project up cold —
with no memory of this campaign and no other briefing — can read in twenty
minutes and come away knowing what happened, what is true now, what is still
broken, and what to do next.

It ends with a **prioritised, argued recommendation** for the next campaign, not
a list of leftovers.

## Why this is a separate task

Eighteen result files is a lot of evidence and nobody reads eighteen files. The
specific thing that gets lost is the **Observations** section: every task was
told to record what it noticed outside its scope and move on, which means real
discoveries are scattered across eighteen documents that will never be opened
again. Consolidating them is most of this task's value.

The other half is that nobody has yet been asked to look at the campaign as a
whole and say what it implies. Every task so far has been deliberately narrow.
This is the one that is allowed to take a position.

## What this document is, and what it is not

**It is a handoff note.** Read `cleanup-2026-09.md` before you start — it is the
document that primed *this* campaign, and yours is its successor. Match its
register: direct, numerate, willing to say "unconfirmed", and honest about which
parts are sketch.

Note how it opens:

> **Status:** draft handoff note, September 2026. Written to prime a fresh
> working context. Not authoritative — the "Plan going forward" section in
> particular is a sketch to be expanded.

**Yours must open the same way, and mean it.** This document is a synthesis and
a set of recommendations. It is explicitly **not** the source of truth. The
durable truth lives in three places, and your document says so near the top:

| Truth | Lives in |
| --- | --- |
| What the system is and what it guarantees | `docs/SPEC.md` |
| What the code actually does | `tests/` |
| What was measured, by whom, when | `docs/campaigns/refactor/results/` |

Someone acting on your recommendations should be able to verify every claim
against one of those three. Make that easy: cite the result file or the test by
name whenever you assert something.

**The consequence for how you write:** it is better to be useful and clearly
labelled than to be cautious and vague. A recommendation you argue for and mark
`RECOMMENDED` is worth more than a hedge. But a guess presented as a finding
poisons the whole document, because a reader who catches one stops trusting all
of it.

