## Confidence labelling

Use three labels throughout, and use them on every non-obvious claim:

| Label | Means |
| --- | --- |
| **MEASURED** | A number or observation taken from a result file or a test. Cite the source. |
| **INFERRED** | A conclusion drawn from measurements. Say which measurements and what the reasoning was. |
| **RECOMMENDED** | Your judgement about what to do. Say what you considered and rejected. |

A section with no labels reads as fact. That is the failure mode to avoid.

## The document — required sections

Write to `docs/campaigns/refactor/handoff-2026-09-refactor.md`. Target **300–500
lines**. Longer is not better; `cleanup-2026-09.md` did its job in 294.

### 1. Status block and what this is

The header, in the register described above. Include: date, the base branch, the
commit SHA at the end of the campaign, and the three-row truth table.

### 2. What the project is

Two paragraphs, self-contained, assuming no prior knowledge. Aurora fine-tuning
for MJO sub-seasonal prediction, the r > 0.5 at 30 days target, the machine, the
data. A reader who has never seen this repo should be able to follow section 10
after reading only this.

### 3. What this campaign did

One table, eighteen rows: task, one-line outcome, final status. Then two or
three paragraphs on the shape of it — what the campaign was for, what the two
gates were, whether they held.

Do not narrate each task. The table is the narration.

### 4. The state now

What a person gets when they clone. Layout, how to install, the one command that
defines done, how to run a smoke test, how to submit a job. Test counts by
category from D2/D3/F2. Whether `uv` works on Perlmutter, plainly, without
euphemism.

### 5. The numbers

Everything MEASURED, in tables. At minimum: B1 vs C4 vs F2 comparisons;
parameter counts by mode; sample counts; static-variable statistics; the
non-finite-gradient skip ratio from F2; peak memory and step time; test counts;
the type-checker exclusion list length.

This is the section a reader scrolls back to. Make it dense and make every row
cite its source.

### 6. What was ruled out

A hypothesis eliminated is worth as much as one confirmed, and this is the
section that stops the next campaign re-running a dead end. Pull from every
result file's "What was ruled out" section.

**B2's verdict belongs here and is the most consequential single line in the
document**: were the July runs affected by the zeroed-statics bug, and therefore
does any prior result on this project mean anything? State it in one sentence,
label it, and point at `docs/findings/2026-09-zeroed-statics.md`.

### 7. What is still broken

Two lists, kept separate because they mean different things.

**Inherited and deliberately not fixed** — from `00_CONTEXT.md` §5: Findings 3,
5 and 6; the PLACEHOLDER `msl` normalisation statistics; small-vs-full Aurora;
`slt_data.nc` on purgeable scratch. For each: what it is, why it was out of
scope, and what it blocks.

**Discovered during the campaign** — this is the Observations harvest. Read the
Observations section of **all eighteen** result files and consolidate. Expect
duplicates across tasks; merge them. Expect some to be trivial; say so and drop
them rather than padding the list. For each survivor: which task noticed it,
what it affects, and whether it is worth acting on.

Also consolidate every **AMBER caveat** from every result file. A caveat is by
definition something unresolved that someone decided not to block on, and they
accumulate invisibly.

### 8. What the campaign got wrong

Planning errors, bad budget estimates, assumptions in `00_`–`04_` that turned out
to be false, tasks that were scoped wrongly, gates that fired or should have.

Specific things to check rather than assume:

- Did the sample-count arithmetic in `03_DOMAIN_PRIORS.md` §1 survive contact
  with real and synthetic data? D1 and D3 both tested it.
- Was the `aurora` / `aurora_mjo` collision real, or was the precaution
  unnecessary? C1 would know.
- Which budgets were badly wrong, in which direction?
- Did any task's "Must not touch" list turn out to be wrong — either too tight
  to do the work, or too loose to prevent a collision?
- Did C4 or F2 catch anything, or did they pass trivially? A gate that has never
  caught anything is worth examining.

Write this plainly and without defensiveness. The next planner is the audience
and the point is to make their plan better.

### 9. Open questions

Every `Q-nn` still unanswered, with what it now blocks — which may have changed
since it was raised. Plus any question the campaign raised and did not record as
a `Q-nn`.

For each, restate the proposed default and say whether the campaign's experience
strengthens or weakens it.

### 10. Recommended next steps

**The section the document exists for.** Everything above is input to this.

Produce a **prioritised sequence**, not a backlog. For each item:

- what it is, in one sentence
- why it is at that position — what it unblocks, or what it depends on
- roughly what it costs
- what evidence supports doing it, cited
- your confidence, and what would change your mind

Then propose the **shape of the next campaign**: a name, a base branch, and
three to five phases with a sentence each. Enough that someone can create the
directory from `_TEMPLATE/` and start filling it in.

Some things you should weigh, in no particular order — this is a prompt for your
judgement, not a ranking to copy out:

- The real `ps` normalisation statistics. `02_UPSTREAM_CONTRACT.md` §4.1 makes
  the case that nothing scientific is trustworthy until these exist; F2's skip
  ratio is the evidence for or against that.
- Findings 3, 5 and 6. Finding 5 needs a **human scientific decision** — the
  physics-informed mode freezes the whole backbone, so the moisture-budget loss
  scores fields whose decoders cannot learn. Do not recommend an implementation
  for something that first needs a decision; recommend the decision.
- Small vs full Aurora, which was chosen under deadline pressure and flagged
  provisional. `03_DOMAIN_PRIORS.md` §7 has the memory numbers, and they
  interact with the gradient-checkpointing crash.
- Whether the ~41k trainable parameters are the right training surface at all.
- The RMM and evaluation code, if C3 reported AMBER on splitting it — 1,637
  lines with no test coverage, in exactly the place a leakage bug would hide.
- `slt_data.nc` on purgeable scratch (Q-07).
- Reducing the type-checker exclusion list.

**Be willing to say which single thing matters most and argue for it.** A
prioritised list where everything is important is not a prioritisation. If you
genuinely cannot separate the top two, say so and give the human the criterion
that would separate them.

