   `00_`–`04_` again — you need to know what was *promised* to judge what was
   delivered.

   **Take notes as you go into the document itself.** Do not read everything and
   then start writing; a task that dies at minute 150 of a 180-minute budget with
   nothing written has destroyed all of its own value.

2. Build the Observations harvest first, before writing prose. A flat list of
   every Observation and every AMBER caveat with its source task. Then merge
   duplicates and drop trivia. This list is the input to sections 7 and 10 and
   it is the thing most likely to be skimped if you leave it until last.

3. Build the numbers tables (section 5). These are transcription, they are
   quick, and having them in front of you makes sections 6–10 much easier to
   write honestly.

4. Write sections 1–9.

5. Write section 10 last, when you have everything. Re-read your own section 7
   and section 8 immediately before writing it.

6. Verify every citation. Every claim labelled MEASURED must name a result file
   or a test that actually exists. Spot-check at least ten, and say in your
   result file which ten and whether they checked out.

7. Add one line to `docs/PROJECT_STATE.md` pointing at this document, under
   **Next action** or immediately below it, so the document is findable after
   this campaign directory is archived. **Change nothing else in that file.**

8. Re-read your document end to end as if you had never seen this project.
   Anything you cannot follow without opening another file is a gap — fix it or
   say in the document that it is a gap.

